  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP as the Implementing Partner will comply with the policies, procedures and practices of the United Nations Security Management System (UNSMS.)</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Local or national precedents set to prioritize the inclusion and empowerment of women and girls</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2018">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Negligible numbers of women included in peace processes in the region especially at the local levels
- WPS challenges are rarely addressed at the national level</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Reprogramming and launch of N-Peace CSO network projects in line with the realities and challenges posed by the COVID-19 pandemic (18).
-Women, Peace and Security advocates in participating countries supported with increased visibility for their work through advocacy efforts</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Successful reprogramming of affected CSOs to respond and advocate for context-specific Women, Peace and Security concerns such as the rise of inter-communal violence in indigenous communities, and women&#x2019;s mediation through new technologies for the prevention of violent extremism. Three online technologies rolled out for women&#x2019;s mediation in Indonesia, Pakistan and Afghanistan to support ongoing WPS efforts. Approximately 40,775 direct beneficiaries recorded in participating countries. 
- 3 online capacity training sessions held to support N-Peace network. Advocacy campaign run together with UNDP Regional Bureau of Arab States, as well as N-Peace awareness reached over 43 million Twitter timeline deliveries with over 8 million users engaged in Phase I of the project (2018-2020).</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Generating lessons from Women, Peace and Security work for future programming.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Women, Peace and Security underwent an external review to inform its peacebuilding work across the region which will be further brainstormed in a regional workshop in November. 2022 TRAC will be used to support strengthening of WPS dimensions of peacebuilding portfolio in 2 UNDP COs (tentatively Nepal, Philippines)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
